+# Flagged cp prompts despite the allow rule — Diagnosis
+
+## TLDR
+- Every `cp -R` in the plan-archive run showed a native Claude Code permission prompt, even though `Bash(cp *)` is allowed and the target is a registered additional directory.
+- The tool-policy hook hands every allow-rule match back to the engine (decision `none`) so that the engine's own checks still run. One of those checks, "cp with flags requires manual approval", asks for approval no matter what the allow rules say. So the handoff ends in a prompt, which breaks the "nothing prompts" closed world.
+- Fix: the hook allows a flagged `cp` itself when every flag is a short cluster of safe letters (never `-t` or a long option) and every path is inside the write roots. It returns `allow` instead of `none`, so the engine's flag check never runs.
+
+## Context
+- **Symptom:** session "Plan archive through September 24th - Checkout" (2026-09-25, 19:55–20:15). Each `cp -R <plans/…-wip-…> <agent-plans/claude-configs/…-done-…>` raised the native prompt "cp command with flags requires manual approval". It prompted in default mode and in acceptEdits mode alike.
+- **Environment:** main checkout of claude-configs, deployed hook `~/.claude/hooks/bin/toolpolicy`, Claude Code 2.1.280 (bundled with Desktop).
+- **What still works:** every read-only Bash call and every `git …` call in the same session was decided by the hook (`source: hook`) without a prompt.
+- **Reproduction:** any flagged `cp` whose paths are inside the write roots and which matches `Bash(cp *)`. Example: `cp -R plans/X ~/GolandProjects/agent-plans/claude-configs/Y`.
+
